(Bug #79 rejects a top-level read written above the variable's own
 declaration. A function body is the case that must NOT be rejected:
 LANGUAGE.md's "Function Scope" says a top-level variable is global and
 can be used inside functions, and a function runs when it is called -
 after the whole file has been read - so a global declared further down
 IS in scope in a body written above it. `bump` is defined above `tally`
 and both reads and writes it; `announce` is defined below `label` and
 reads it. Neither may become an error.)

To bump.
  Set tally to tally add 1.

a number called tally is 0.
a text called label is "hello".

To announce.
  Print label.

bump.
bump.
Print tally.
announce.
